[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m tests, which is why all three streams are asserted separately.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Branch coverage of the C program
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m A coverage-instrumented copy of the C source (built in `/tmp`, so `c_src/` was
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m left untouched) was run under the enumerated inputs:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m src/main.c       Lines executed: 100.00% of 250    Branches taken: 100.00% of 60
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m src/inventory.c  Lines executed:  81.91% of  94
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main.c` is fully covered — every `if`, every `switch` arm, every early
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `return`. The uncovered `inventory.c` lines are **unreachable from `main()`**,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m so no stdin input can distinguish them:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `find_expensive_items` — declared in `inventory.h`, never called. Dead code in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the executable.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - the `!items || items->size == 0` guard in `calculate_inventory_stats` —
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `demo_inventory_array` always pushes 10 items first.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - the `!orders || orders->size == 0` guard in `calculate_order_stats` —
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `demo_order_list` always appends 8 orders first.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - the `found == 0` arm of `find_items_by_category` — `main` only ever queries
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `"Electronics"` and `"Furniture"`, and both match items.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - the container operations `main` never uses: `array_*_get` (for most types),
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `array_*_clear`, `list_*_prepend`, `list_*_clear`, `list_*_size`, and the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `create`/`push` variants for types only one container instantiates.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These were checked by reading the Rust translation instead of by execution; see
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m "Structural divergences" below.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Input classes enumerated
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Each row is asserted on all three of stdout, stderr and exit status.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Class | Inputs | C behavior being pinned |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | --- | --- | --- |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Empty input | 0 bytes, `/dev/null`, closed fd | `fgets` returns `NULL` on the first call → loop breaks → exit 0 after the banner and one menu |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | One item | `1`…`5` alone | one demo, then EOF → exit 0 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Maximum the code handles | `6` (runs all five demos), `6` ×25 (~2.7 MB of stdout) | crosses stdio's 4 KB block buffer many times |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Early `return` | `7`, `7` with trailing input | prints `Goodbye!` and returns 0 *without* consuming the rest of stdin |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `default:` arm | `0`, `8`, `9`, `100`, `-1`, `-2147483648` | `Invalid choice`, loop continues |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `sscanf != 1` arm | `abc`, `!!!`, ` `, `\t`, `-`, `+`, `.`, blank lines | `Invalid input`, loop continues |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Leading whitespace | `   3`, `\t4`, `\t\v\f 4` | `%d` skips all six C whitespace characters |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Trailing garbage | `3abc`, `3 4 5`, `  3  `, `1;drop` | `%d` stops at the first non-digit and still returns 1 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Signs | `+5`, `+7`, `-0`, `007` | `%d` accepts an optional sign and leading zeros |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Non-decimal forms | `0x3`, `3.9`, `.5`, `1e3`, `1_000` | `%d` is decimal-only: `0x3`→0, `3.9`→3, `.5`→no conversion |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Overflow / truncation | `2147483648`, `-2147483649`, `4294967296`, `4294967303`, `2^63`, `-2^63`, 26 nines, 400 nines | glibc converts as `long` (saturating at `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN`) then truncates to `int` — e.g. 400 nines → `LONG_MAX` → `(int)-1` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `fgets` buffer split | 254 spaces + `17`, 255 spaces + `7`, 254 `x` + `7`, exactly 255 bytes then `7`, 300-byte line, 600 digits | `fgets` stops after 255 bytes; the remainder of the line becomes the *next* loop iteration's input |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | No trailing newline | `3`, `7`, `8`, `abc`, `6\n1` | `fgets` returns the partial line, the next call returns `NULL` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Embedded NUL | `\0` + `5`, `5` + `\0` + `6`, `\0` alone | `fgets` copies the bytes, but the C string `sscanf` sees ends at the NUL |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Non-UTF-8 bytes | `\x80`, `\xff\xfe`, `\xc3\x28\xa0\xa1`, U+FF13 (fullwidth 3) | input is never echoed, so raw bytes must be tolerated, and a fullwidth digit is *not* a digit |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | CR / CRLF | `3\r\n7\r\n`, `3\r7\r`, `\r3` | `\r` is whitespace to `%d`, but not a line terminator to `fgets` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Long loops | 500 invalid lines, 500 invalid choices, full menu walks, alternating valid/invalid | prompt/demo/error text interleaving across many iterations |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Randomized differential fuzzing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 2,900 total trials (400 + 2,500, fixed seeds) over random token sequences drawn
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m from the classes above, with ~6% of trials replaced by uniformly random byte
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m strings up to 800 bytes. Zero mismatches on stdout, stderr or exit status.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Structural divergences that are not behavioral divergences
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These are places where the Rust code does not look like the C but cannot be
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m distinguished by running the program. They are recorded so a future reader does
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m not have to re-derive why they are safe.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 1. **Dropped NULL guards.** `calculate_inventory_stats`, `calculate_order_stats`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `find_items_by_category` and `find_expensive_items` all begin with `!items`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    (and `!category`) checks in C. The Rust versions take `&Array<T>` / `&List<T>`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    / `&str`, which cannot be null, so the checks are absent. `main` never passes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    null, so the guarded `return` is unreachable in the C executable too.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `array_*_create` returning `NULL` on allocation failure is likewise not
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    modeled; the Rust code aborts on OOM instead.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 2. **`printf("%s", buf)` on fixed-size `char[]`.** `item.name`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `item.category` and `order.customer_name` are `char` arrays written by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `strncpy` and are printed up to the first NUL. The Rust code keeps them as
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `[u8; N]` and writes the bytes before the first NUL with `stdio::out_raw`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    rather than going through `Display`, so no UTF-8 transcoding or escaping can
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    alter them. All the literals in this program are ASCII, but the raw path
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    makes that irrelevant.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 3. **`strncpy` truncation is preserved.** `strncpy_terminated::<N>` copies at
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    most `N - 1` bytes, zero-fills the rest, and always leaves a NUL — matching
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `strncpy(dst, src, N-1); dst[N-1] = '\0';`. No name in this program is long
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    enough to truncate, so this is untested by execution.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 4. **The linked list is an index arena.** `List<T>` stores nodes in a `Vec` with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `head`/`tail` as `Option<usize>` instead of raw pointers, and `size()`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    returns `nodes.len()`. `LIST_FOREACH` walks the `next` chain from `head`, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `ListIter` does the same, so iteration order matches. This only coincides
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    with `nodes.len()` because `main` never calls `prepend`, `clear` or any
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    removal — `prepend` would still be correct (it links `head`, and `size` still
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    equals the node count), but a hypothetical remove operation would not be.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 5. **`array_*_capacity` bookkeeping is mirrored but unobservable.** `Array<T>`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    keeps an explicit `capacity` field and doubles it when `size >= capacity`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    like the C macro, even though nothing prints capacity. `create(0)` bumping to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    16 is preserved for the same reason.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 6. **Deliberately preserved C oddities.**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    - `calculate_inventory_stats` seeds `max_price = 0.0` but `min_price =
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m      items->data[0].price`. The asymmetry is kept: an inventory of only
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m      negative-priced items would report `Most expensive item: $0.00`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    - `calculate_order_stats` uses `min_order = -1.0` as a sentinel and tests
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m      `min_order < 0`, so a genuinely negative order total would be treated as
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m      "not yet set". Kept as-is.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    - `total_value / total_items` in `calculate_inventory_stats` divides by an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m      `int` sum of quantities, which is `0` when every quantity is 0 — that is a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m      float division producing `inf`/`nan`, not a trap. Kept as-is.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    - `int sum` in `demo_integer_containers` is accumulated with `wrapping_add`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    152[0m:[38;5;10m[49m      and `long long product` with `wrapping_mul`, so signed overflow wraps as
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    153[0m:[38;5;10m[49m      gcc's two's-complement codegen does rather than panicking in debug builds.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    154[0m:[38;5;10m[49m      Neither actually overflows with the hard-coded values.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    155[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    156[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 7. **Non-ASCII literals are spelled as escapes.** The banner box-drawing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    157[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    characters (U+2554, U+2550, U+2557, U+2551, U+255A, U+255D) and the degree
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    158[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    sign in `Minimum: %.1f°C` are written as `\u{...}` in the Rust source. Rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    159[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    encodes them as UTF-8 into the output buffer, which is byte-identical to the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    160[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    UTF-8 literals in `main.c`. Verified by the byte-for-byte match on menu
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    161[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    choices 2 and 6.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    162[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    163[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 8. **Output buffering.** C's `stdout` is block-buffered when redirected, so
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    164[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `Choice: ` is not flushed before `fgets` blocks. The Rust `stdio` module
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    165[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    buffers into a `Vec<u8>` and flushes before each `fgets` and before exit.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    166[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    The flush *timing* differs; the byte *sequence* on stdout does not, and only
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    167[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    the sequence is compared. The explicit flush before `std::process::exit(0)`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    168[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    on menu choice 7 is required — `exit` does not run destructors.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    169[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    170[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 9. **Unused source files.** `translation/src/cio.rs` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    171[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `translation/src/cstr.rs` are not declared as modules in `main.rs` and are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    172[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    therefore not compiled. They duplicate functionality that now lives in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    173[0m:[38;5;10m[49m    `stdio.rs` and `inventory.rs`. They are dead weight, not a behavioral risk.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    174[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    175[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Limits of this verification
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    176[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    177[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Allocation failure paths (`malloc`/`realloc` returning `NULL`, and the `-1`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    178[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   returns from `array_*_push` / `list_*_append` that `main` discards anyway) are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    179[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   not exercised in either program.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    180[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - `find_expensive_items` and the empty-container / no-match branches listed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    181[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   under "Branch coverage" cannot be reached through stdin. Their Rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    182[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   counterparts were checked by reading, not by running.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    183[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Only stdin content varies. The C program takes no command-line arguments and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    184[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   reads no environment variables or files, so there is no other input surface.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    185[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Comparison was done on x86-64 Linux with gcc and glibc. The `%d` overflow
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    186[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   behavior in item 6 of the input table is glibc's `strtol`-style saturation; a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    187[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   different libc could differ, and the Rust code matches glibc.
